    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Show that the line passing through $(a,0)$ and $(0,b)$, where $a\\ne0$ and $b\\ne0$, has equation $\\dfrac xa+\\dfrac yb=1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$m=-\\frac ba$, so $y=-\\frac bax+b$, giving $\\frac xa+\\frac yb=1$.\n\nUse the gradient of the two intercepts and the $y$-intercept $b$.",
      "worked": "The gradient is\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{b-0}{0-a}\\\\m&=-\\frac ba\\end{align*}$$\nThe $y$-intercept is $b$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=-\\frac bax+b\\\\\\frac yb&=-\\frac xa+1\\\\\\frac xa+\\frac yb&=1\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 43
